00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
In the early days of deep learning, people used to worry a lot about the optimization algorithm getting stuck in bad local optima.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.000
But as the theory of deep learning has advanced, our understanding of local optima is also changing.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Let me show you how we now think about local optima and problems with the optimization problem in deep learning.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.000
So this was a picture people used to have in mind when they worried about local optima.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Maybe you're trying to optimize some set of parameters, we call them w1 and w2, and the height of the surface is the cost function.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.000
So in this picture, it looks like there are a lot of local optima in all those places,

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:46.000
and it'd be easy for gradient descent or one of the other algorithms to get stuck on a local optimum rather than find its way to a global optimum.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:56.000
It turns out that if you're plotting a figure like this in two dimensions, then it's easy to create plots like this with a lot of different local optima.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.000
And these very low dimensional plots used to gather intuition. But this intuition isn't actually correct.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:10.000
It turns out if you train a neural network, most points of zero gradients are not local optima like points like this.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Instead, most points of zero gradients in cost function are actually saddle points.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:25.000
So that's a point where the zero gradients, again, this is maybe w1, w2, and the height is the value of the cost function j.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:31.000
But informally, a function of very high dimensional space, if the gradient is zero, then in each direction,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:37.000
it can either be a convex light function or a concave light function.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:46.000
And if you're in, say, a 20,000 dimensional space, then for it to be a local optima, all 20,000 directions need to look like this.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:52.000
And so the chance of that happening is maybe very small, maybe 2 to the minus 20,000.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Instead, you're much more likely to get some directions where the curve bends up like so,

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000
as well as some directions where the curve function is bending down rather than have them all bend upwards.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:13.000
So that's why in very high dimensional spaces, you're actually much more likely to run into a saddle point like that shown on the right than the local optima.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Oh, and as for why this surface is called a saddle point, if you can picture maybe this is the sort of saddle you put on a horse, right?

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:28.000
So maybe if this is a horse, I guess there's a head of a horse, there's the eye of a horse, you know, I guess.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:33.000
And right, well, not a great drawing of a horse, but you get the idea.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Then you, the rider, would sit here in the saddle.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:47.000
So that's why this point here with the derivative is zero, that point is called a saddle point.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:53.000
It's really the point on the saddle where you would sit, I guess, and that happens to have, you know, derivative zero.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:01.000
And so one of the lessons we learned in history of deep learning is that a lot of our intuitions about low dimensional spaces, like what you can plot on the left,

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:07.000
they really don't transfer to the very high dimensional spaces that our learning algorithms are operating over.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:14.000
Because if you have 20,000 parameters, then J is via function over a 20,000 dimensional vector,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.000
and you're much more likely to see saddle points than local optimum.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.000
If local optimal aren't a problem, then what is a problem?

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.000
It turns out that plateaus can really slow down learning.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.000
And a plateau is a region where the derivative is close to zero for a long time.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:38.000
So if you're here, then gradient descent will move down the surface.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.000
And because the gradient is zero or near zero, the surface is quite flat.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:51.000
You can actually take a very long time, you know, to slowly find your way to maybe this point on the plateau.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:58.000
And then because of a random perturbation to the left or right, maybe then finally, I'm going to switch pen colors for clarity.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Your algorithm can then find its way off the plateau.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.000
But that to take this very long, slow path before it's found its way here, and it could get off this plateau.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:14.000
So the takeaways from this video are first, you're actually pretty unlikely to get stuck in bad local optima,

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.000
so long as you're trading a reasonably large neural network.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:23.000
So you have a lot of parameters, and the cost function J is defined over a relatively high dimensional space.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.000
But second, that plateaus are a problem, and they can actually make learning pretty slow.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:35.000
And this is where algorithms like momentum or RMS prop or add them can really help you learning algorithm as well.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:47.000
And these are scenarios where most of skated optimization algorithms, such as Adam, can actually speed up the rate at which you could move down the plateau and then get off the plateau.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:52.000
So because neural networks are solving optimization problems over such high dimensional spaces,

00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:00.000
to be honest, I don't think anyone has great intuitions about what these spaces really look like, and our understanding of them is still evolving.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.000
But I hope this gives you some better intuition about the challenges that the optimization algorithms may face.

